WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.219
The same idea at every provider.

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:04.952
Tool calling is not a Claude-specific trick.

3
00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:08.219
The vocabulary changes; the protocol does not.

4
00:00:08.499 --> 00:00:19.315
OpenAI’s function calling guide describes the same five-step flow: send tools, receive a tool call, run your code, send the output back, receive the final answer.

5
00:00:19.595 --> 00:00:29.216
In its Responses API, a call arrives as a function_call item with a call_id, and you return a function_call_output item with the same id.

6
00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:36.067
Parallel calls are on by default, and a parallel_tool_calls flag limits the model to zero or one call.

7
00:00:36.347 --> 00:00:43.408
Google’s Gemini docs describe function calls returned with a name and arguments, which you execute and send back.

8
00:00:43.688 --> 00:00:58.301
They cover parallel calling (several functions in one turn) and compositional calling (a chain, where one call’s output feeds the next), and modes that let the model decide, force a call, forbid calls, or enforce schema adherence.

9
00:00:58.581 --> 00:01:06.261
xAI’s Grok docs follow the same request, execute, return pattern, with parallel calls enabled by default.

10
00:01:06.541 --> 00:01:08.269
Two practical consequences.

11
00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:15.824
First, your tool implementations and your gating logic are portable; only the adapter that translates blocks differs.

12
00:01:16.104 --> 00:01:18.173
Second, behavior is not portable.

13
00:01:18.453 --> 00:01:27.541
How eagerly a model calls tools, how often it calls them in parallel, and how well it recovers from errors all vary between model families and versions.

14
00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:31.533
When you switch models, re-run your tool evals rather than assuming.
